{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lilac-energetic-grosbeak","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Parent\n\nEpic `77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec`. This is the net-mechanism half of deliverable 3 — the RED\nPROOF for the `project.net-mechanism` criterion created by `fe94-2e22-ea9e-46f7`.\n\nSplit from `fe94` because carrying the criterion and its corpus in one description exceeds\nthe 8,000-character plan-review admission cap `verify.max_ticket_description_chars`\n(measured: 15,612 characters combined, against an unconfigured schema default of 8,000 at\n`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:52`).\n\n## Why firing proof cannot live in a unit test\n\nMeasured from the sibling criterion's test module: `tests/unit/test_project_portability_criterion.py`\nnever executes model judgment. It asserts activation, routing metadata, front-matter keys,\nprompt resolution, and the presence of required headings in the rubric body — all\nstructural. A unit test asserting \"this plan is scored as a finding\" could only do so\nagainst a canned score, which proves the fixture and not the criterion.\n\nSo `fe94` carries only structural tests, and ALL firing behaviour is proven here, in the\ncalibration corpus — the same place `project.portability` proves its own.\n\n## The corpus\n\n`.rebar/evals/plan-review-project-net-mechanism.eval.yaml`, modeled on\n`.rebar/evals/plan-review-project-portability.eval.yaml` (215 lines, read from the working\ntree). Config: `epochs: 3`, `gate: at_least(2)`, `coverage_threshold: 1.0`, and a\ndeterministic `emits_valid_findings` scorer. Dataset is BALANCED — 4 must-fire and 4\nmust-not-fire cases, each carrying `id`, `corpus`, `criterion`, `expect`, `note`, `input`.\n\nBalance is the point: a corpus of only must-fire cases cannot distinguish a working\ncriterion from one that fires on everything.\n\n### Must-fire\n\n- `NETMECH-F1` — the red-proof case, below.\n- `NETMECH-F2` — adds a `feature_flag`, gives a reason, names no retirement.\n- `NETMECH-F3` — adds an `autouse_fixture`, framed as \"small enough not to count\".\n- `NETMECH-F4` — adds a `ci_gate` and a `config_key`, retires neither.\n\n### Must-not-fire\n\n- `NETMECH-N1` — adds an `env_var` and names the `config_key` it retires.\n- `NETMECH-N2` — adds a `lock` and states why no retirement is possible.\n- `NETMECH-N3` — adds no mechanism of any of the seven kinds.\n- `NETMECH-N4` — only removes mechanisms.\n\n`N1` and `N2` exist because the criterion's threshold has two independent escape conditions\n(a named retirement, or a stated justification); a corpus exercising only one would leave\nthe other unproven.\n\n## The red-proof referent\n\n`NETMECH-F1` derives from a change that actually landed. `2041-75d6-2a2f-4be9` added the\n`env_var` `REBAR_LLM_EMBEDDING_MODEL`, admitted by the marker at\n`src/rebar/llm/config_readers.py:152`; the name is consequently ABSENT from\n`.github/mechanism-baseline.json`, because the marker admits it so it never enters the\nbaseline. Nothing was retired alongside it. Both facts were verified directly.\n\nStated precisely so the case is not mistaken for a quotation: `2041`'s plan TEXT was not\nread. What was measured is the landed OUTCOME — a mechanism added, admitted by marker, no\nretirement recorded. `F1`'s `input` is a faithful reconstruction of that outcome as plan\nprose, not an excerpt of `2041`, and `F1`'s `note` records that distinction along with the\nmarker line as its provenance.\n\nThis is what makes the corpus a red proof rather than a self-fulfilling test: the case is\ndrawn from a change the existing ratchet passed and this criterion should have caught.\n\n## Files this change touches\n\n1. `.rebar/evals/plan-review-project-net-mechanism.eval.yaml` — NEW, the corpus.\n2. `tests/unit/test_project_net_mechanism_eval.py` — NEW, offline shape checks on the\n   spec, modeled on the portability eval test. Runs no model.\n\n## Portability and provider neutrality\n\nThe offline shape checks parse YAML and assert structure; they touch no network, no CI\nenvironment, and no provider. They pass with no CI provider configured and with no model\nAPI key present.\n\nThe eval spec's `model` key names the model the corpus was CALIBRATED against. It does not\nconstrain which provider or model runs the criterion in production — routing selects that\nindependently — so this corpus does not narrow rebar's provider support.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nEach names one deliverable; none bundles two with \"and\".\n\nOffline, in `tests/unit/test_project_net_mechanism_eval.py`:\n\n- [ ] The spec parses as YAML.\n- [ ] The spec declares `criterion: project.net-mechanism`.\n- [ ] The dataset contains exactly 4 cases with `expect: fire`.\n- [ ] The dataset contains exactly 4 cases with `expect: no-fire`.\n- [ ] Every case carries all six of `id`, `corpus`, `criterion`, `expect`, `note`, `input`.\n- [ ] Every case `id` is unique.\n- [ ] The spec declares `epochs: 3`.\n- [ ] The spec declares `gate: at_least(2)`.\n- [ ] The spec declares `coverage_threshold: 1.0`.\n- [ ] `NETMECH-F1`'s `note` cites `src/rebar/llm/config_readers.py:152`.\n- [ ] The test module executes no model call, asserted by the module importing no runner.\n\nSemantic, operator-attested rather than a test:\n\n- [ ] `rebar criteria eval project.net-mechanism --runs 3` meets the declared gate on all\n      8 cases, with the run output recorded as a comment on this ticket.\n\nGate:\n\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0.\n\n## Dependency\n\nDepends on `fe94-2e22-ea9e-46f7`: the rubric must exist before the corpus can be evaluated\nagainst it. Recorded as a `depends_on` link, so `ready` will not surface this ticket until\n`fe94` closes.","id":"39bc-072e-9ebf-461d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Calibrate project.net-mechanism with an 8-case plan-review eval corpus"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793654207404890844,"uuid":"2b289d11-bb6e-4162-a8a6-c2319cc1d901"}